<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    ==========================================================================
    DnnMigration.Infrastructure - the Infrastructure layer (AAP 0.4.3/0.5.1.3)
    ==========================================================================

    A CLASS LIBRARY, deliberately built on Microsoft.NET.Sdk and never on the
    web SDK. This layer persists and secures; it does not host HTTP.

    WHAT IT REPLACES
    The whole legacy data-access and security stack, none of which can be
    ported, only replaced:

      * Library/Components/Providers/Data/DataProvider.vb - a 397-line abstract
        class exposing 269 MustOverride members behind a reflection-resolved
        Shared singleton, DataProvider.Instance(). It becomes nine narrow,
        aggregate-shaped repository contracts declared in Domain and resolved
        by constructor injection.

      * Library/Providers/DataProviders/SqlDataProvider/SqlDataProvider.vb -
        1,410 lines invoking 245 distinct stored procedures built as the string
        DatabaseOwner & ObjectQualifier & "<ProcName>" (ExecuteReader x123,
        ExecuteNonQuery x95, ExecuteScalar x37, ExecuteDataset x3, plus four
        raw text-command sites). The helper type behind every one of those
        calls ships ONLY as the source-less binary under
        Library/Components/DataAccessBlock/bin/, so there is literally nothing
        to port - which is why EF Core is mandatory here rather than a shim
        (AAP 0.7.7).

      * Library/Providers/MembershipProviders/DataProvider/SqlDataProvider.vb -
        a second, independent provider stack: 343 lines contributing 40 further
        procedures through a helper whose body is the identical concatenation.
        The core stack contributes only 4 User and 0 Role procedures, so both
        stacks are mandatory reading; the combined reachable surface is roughly
        289 procedures.

      * A reversibly-encrypted credential store. Website/release.config
        registers the legacy membership provider with passwordFormat set to
        Encrypted and password retrieval enabled, against a 3DES key committed
        to source control in plain text. That is replaced by one-way hashing,
        and password retrieval is deliberately not carried forward
        (AAP 0.7.5.5).

    POSITION IN THE REFERENCE GRAPH - Rule T1, "layer inward only" (AAP 0.1.2.2)

        Domain          ->  references nothing at all
        Application     ->  Domain
        Infrastructure  ->  Domain AND Application        <== this project
        Api             ->  Application + Infrastructure

    The reference on Application is MANDATORY, not incidental. This project
    IMPLEMENTS two interfaces that are declared in
    DnnMigration.Application.Abstractions rather than in Domain:
      - ITokenService is implemented by Security/JwtTokenService.cs, together
        with Security/RefreshTokenStore.cs;
      - IModuleBusinessControllerFactory is implemented by
        Services/ModuleBusinessControllerFactory.cs;
    and it consumes JwtOptions, PortalOptions, CachingOptions and
    PasswordPolicyOptions from DnnMigration.Application.Options through
    IOptions<T>. That is exactly the "(and Application for the interfaces it
    implements)" clause of Rule T1. Because the direction is expressed as
    project references, a layering violation is a build failure rather than a
    review finding.

    Never reference DnnMigration.Api from here - Api already references this
    project, so it would invert the graph and create a cycle. Never reference
    either test project: production code does not depend on tests. Never
    reference a legacy project under Library/ or Website/: those trees are
    read-only reference inputs and must stay byte-identical (AAP 0.9.5).

    PACKAGE OWNERSHIP (AAP 0.6.1)
    This project owns EVERY Entity Framework Core, SQL client,
    password-hashing and health-check reference in the solution - and nothing
    else. Deliberately owned elsewhere, so do not restate them here:
      - JWT bearer authentication, OpenAPI/Swagger generation, API versioning
        and structured logging belong to DnnMigration.Api;
      - request validation belongs to DnnMigration.Application and reaches this
        project transitively;
      - the in-memory, file-based and container-backed test database providers
        belong to DnnMigration.IntegrationTests - shipping one of them here
        would put a test provider into production.
    No convention-based object-mapping package is taken anywhere in the
    solution: mappers are hand-written so mapping errors surface at compile
    time (AAP 0.4.3). No ASP.NET Core MVC package appears here, and never a
    framework reference to the ASP.NET Core shared framework - that would drag
    the entire web stack into a class library.

    DnnDbContext is internal to this assembly (Rule T3); Application services
    see repository interfaces only. No assembly-visibility attribute is
    declared here.

    BUILD POLICY IS INHERITED - DO NOT DUPLICATE OR OVERRIDE IT
    backend/Directory.Build.props is imported implicitly by MSBuild walking
    upward from this directory and is the single source of TargetFramework
    (net8.0), LangVersion (12.0), Nullable, ImplicitUsings,
    TreatWarningsAsErrors, NoWarn, GenerateDocumentationFile and
    EnforceCodeStyleInBuild, together with the solution-wide determinism,
    analyser-level and package-audit settings (AAP 0.6.5). This project
    therefore declares NO property group at all: restating an inherited
    property creates two places to change one decision, and overriding one
    would weaken Validation Gate 1, which builds the solution in the Release
    configuration with warnings promoted to errors via the warnaserror switch.
    (Flag literals are spelled out in prose throughout these comments because
    XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment.)

    AssemblyName and RootNamespace are omitted on purpose - both default from
    the file name to DnnMigration.Infrastructure. Contrast the legacy
    Library/DotNetNuke.Library.vbproj, which pins an assembly name of
    DotNetNuke (L13) alongside an EMPTY root namespace (L25-L26); that is
    precisely the confusion being avoided. The legacy project also declared a
    lax warning policy (warnings-as-errors off, warning level 1, empty
    suppression list at L50-L53 and L71-L74), assembly references with hint
    paths into committed bin folders (L78-L106), twenty-five project-level
    namespace imports (L107-L134) and one compile item per source file (L136+).
    Every one of those patterns is intentionally absent: the SDK globs **/*.cs,
    and namespaces come from ImplicitUsings plus explicit using directives.

    Suppression is bounded to exactly CS8618 and CS1591, and only in
    Directory.Build.props. NO OTHER WARNING MAY EVER BE SUPPRESSED, here or
    anywhere (AAP 0.6.3). If this project emits any other warning once warnings
    are promoted to errors, FIX THE CODE - never add a suppression to this file.
    ==========================================================================
  -->

  <ItemGroup>

    <!--
      The 8.0.29 band is not arbitrary. It matches the installed shared
      framework exactly - Microsoft.NETCore.App 8.0.29 and
      Microsoft.AspNetCore.App 8.0.29 on SDK 8.0.423 (AAP 0.1.2.4). Every
      version below is exact-pinned: no floating range, no wildcard and no
      moving tag anywhere in the solution (AAP 0.8.3).
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      Relational primitives consumed by the 21 IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>
      classes under Persistence/Configurations - ToTable("<LegacyTable>", "dbo"),
      HasColumnName, HasKey, HasMaxLength, IsRequired, ValueGeneratedOnAdd.
      Rule T4 keeps the legacy schema immutable, so table and column binding is
      declared here instead of being generated as DDL.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      Provider for the existing, unaltered DotNetNuke SQL Server database. The
      legacy provider registration observed in Website/release.config uses an
      empty object qualifier and "dbo" as the database owner, and those are the
      mapping defaults the configurations bind to.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      Design-time support for the intentionally EMPTY baseline migration
      (Rule T4: __EFMigrationsHistory is seeded without altering any existing
      table) and for Persistence/DnnDbContextFactory.cs.

      KEEP THIS REFERENCE PLAIN - do not add PrivateAssets=all, which is the
      shape the stock template uses for analyser-only packages. Measured from
      the restore graph: with PrivateAssets=all a referencing project receives
      NO Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design library at all, whereas the plain
      form flows lib/net8.0/Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design.dll into that
      project's compile closure. This matters because the CLI resolves
      design-time services from the STARTUP project, and DnnMigration.Api does
      not declare this package itself - it receives it only by transitive flow
      from here. Severing that flow reproduces the familiar "your startup
      project does not reference Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" failure
      and blocks creating the baseline migration.
      (For the record, IDesignTimeDbContextFactory<T> and IDesignTimeServices
      are themselves declared in Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.dll, so the
      factory would still COMPILE either way; the asset flow above, not
      compile-time visibility, is the reason for the plain form.)

      The separate EF Core command-line tooling package is deliberately not
      referenced: it is absent from AAP 0.6.1 and dotnet-ef is installed as a
      global tool.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      EXPLICIT ANTI-DOWNGRADE PIN. Without this line the SQL Server provider
      resolves Microsoft.Data.SqlClient 5.1.7 transitively. The pin was verified
      to change the resolved graph and is deliberate hardening to the 5.2.x
      line. DO NOT REMOVE IT BECAUSE IT LOOKS REDUNDANT.

      The AAP 0.8.3 enterprise baseline names this pin directly, as "an explicit
      client-library pin to prevent a proven transitive downgrade". Confirm the
      effect by listing this project's package graph with transitive
      dependencies included ("dotnet list package", with the
      include-transitive switch) and searching the output for
      Microsoft.Data.SqlClient: it must report 5.2.3 and never 5.1.7.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="5.2.3" />

    <!--
      One-way password hashing for Security/BcryptPasswordHasher.cs. A security
      requirement, not a convenience: it replaces reversible 3DES storage whose
      key is committed to the legacy configuration. Existing credentials cannot
      be verified against a one-way hash, so the documented path is a re-hash on
      first successful login with administrative reset as the fallback, and the
      legacy password policy (minimum length 7, no required non-alphanumeric
      character, non-unique email permitted) is preserved verbatim rather than
      tightened during a migration (AAP 0.7.5.5).
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="BCrypt.Net-Next" Version="4.0.3" />

    <!--
      Database probe behind the anonymous /health endpoint, surfaced by
      HealthChecks/DatabaseHealthCheck.cs. Gate-critical: docker-compose.yml
      gates the frontend service on "condition: service_healthy", so a /health
      that is unreachable or requires authentication stops the frontend
      container from ever starting even when both images build cleanly. The
      Alpine runtime image probes it with wget in spider mode, not curl.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AspNetCore.HealthChecks.SqlServer" Version="8.0.2" />

  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Domain\DnnMigration.Domain.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Application\DnnMigration.Application.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
